.Dd $Mdocdate: September 18 2026 $
.Dt FSUNABA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fsunaba
.Nd execute X applications in a sandboxed X display
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl A
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl G
.Op Fl d Ar display
.Op Fl s Ar width Ns x Ns Ar height
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Op Fl w
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility provides a rudimentary sandboxed environment, both user and
.Xr X 7
session, to run an X application in.
The intent is to limit access to the user's own files and prevent the
X application from accessing
.Xr XEvents 3
events destined for other applications, especially keyboard input.
.Pp
The sandbox consists of two parts:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
A separate, less privileged local user account that runs the application,
restricting access to the invoking user's files according to their permissions.
.It
A nested
.Xr Xephyr 1
X server rendered into a window on the host display, isolating the application
from the host X session.
.El
.Pp
IMPORTANT:
.Nm
is a proof of concept and in no way guarantees that it will prevent access to
your data.
The sandbox does not restrict network, audio, or clipboard access by default,
and the application runs with the full privileges of the sandbox user.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl A
Run the application as the amnesic sandbox user and erase the contents of that
user's home directory before and after the session.
The amnesic user is configured with the
.Ev FSUNABA_AMNESIC_USER
environment variable and must first be created with
.Ic make install-amnesic-user .
The option takes precedence over
.Fl u .
.It Fl d Ar display
Render the sandbox display into the given host
.Xr X 7
display.
Defaults to the value of the
.Ev DISPLAY
environment variable.
.It Fl G
Pass the
.Fl no-host-grab
option to
.Xr Xephyr 1 ,
so the sandbox window does not grab the keyboard and mouse.
.It Fl h
Print a usage message and exit.
.It Fl r
Pass the
.Fl resizeable
option to
.Xr Xephyr 1 ,
making the sandbox window resizeable.
.It Fl s Ar width Ns x Ns Ar height
Set the sandbox display resolution in pixels, for example
.Ar 1280x1024 .
Defaults to 1024x768, except for applications with their own default, as
described in
.Sx APPLICATION DEFAULTS .
.It Fl u Ar user
Run the application as the named sandbox user.
Defaults to the
.Cm fsunaba
user.
The user must already exist.
.It Fl v
Show verbose output.
.It Fl w
Run a window manager inside the sandbox, before the application.
The manager is
.Cm cwm
by default and can be changed with the
.Ev FSUNABA_WM
environment variable.
A window manager is needed by applications that expect another client to give
them the input focus, such as the popup menus of Firefox-based browsers; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.El
.Pp
.Nm
creates a private temporary directory with mode 700 to hold its X authority
file and the diagnostic output of
.Xr Xephyr 1 ,
and removes it together with all authentication cookies when the application
exits or the utility is interrupted.
Each invocation gets its own X display:
.Nm
picks the lowest display number that is free on the host and starts
.Xr Xephyr 1
on it, so
.Xr Xephyr 1
locks that display.
A second invocation started while the first is running therefore uses a
different display, and neither application can read the other's windows or
inject events into them.
.Pp
The application receives a minimal environment consisting only of
.Ev DISPLAY ,
.Ev HOME ,
.Ev LOGNAME ,
.Ev USER ,
and
.Ev PATH ,
its arguments are passed through unchanged, and it is started with its home
directory as the working directory, since the invoking user's working directory
is usually not readable by the sandbox user.
The root cursor is set to the usual arrow, because a popup window that sets no
cursor of its own would otherwise inherit the server default, a large X.
.Sh APPLICATION DEFAULTS
.Nm
appends window geometry options for applications it recognizes, so that the
application window fits the sandbox display:
.Bl -tag -width "ungoogled-chromium" -compact
.It chrome , chromium , ungoogled-chromium
The
.Ic -window-size=width,height
and
.Ic --window-position=0,0
options.
.It firefox
The
.Ic -width
and
.Ic -height
options.
.It tor-browser
The
.Ic -width
and
.Ic -height
options, and, unless a size was requested with
.Fl s
or the
.Ev WIDTH
and
.Ev HEIGHT
environment variables, a 1000x1000 sandbox display.
Tor Browser opens a 1000x1000 window by default and letterboxes its content to
multiples of 200x100 pixels, so this keeps the browser's default window size
and does not change its fingerprint.
.El
.Pp
Applications not listed above receive no geometry options.
.Pp
With
.Fl A ,
the amnesic user's home directory is emptied before the application starts, so
each session begins with an empty home, and it is emptied again when the session
ends.
Files left with restrictive permissions are made writable first, and
.Nm
reports an error if anything survives the erase.
.Nm
refuses to run amnesic mode as root, against the invoking user, against
.Cm root ,
or when the amnesic home is not under
.Pa /home .
Data written outside the home directory, such as in
.Pa /tmp ,
is not erased.
.Pp
.Nm
is a
.Xr ksh 1
script and requires the
.Xr ksh 1
that ships with
.Ox .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The following environment variables affect
.Nm
behavior:
.Bl -tag -width "FSUNABA_XEPHYR_OPTS"
.It Ev VERBOSE
Show verbose output if set to
.Sq true ,
otherwise suppress it.
Defaults to
.Sq false .
.It Ev FSUNABA_WM
Window manager to run when the
.Fl w
option is given.
Defaults to
.Cm cwm .
.It Ev FSUNABA_WM
Window manager to run inside the sandbox when the
.Fl w
option is given.
Defaults to
.Cm cwm .
.It Ev FSUNABA_HOST_DISPLAY
Host
.Xr X 7
display to render the sandbox into.
Overridden by the
.Fl d
option.
.It Ev FSUNABA_USER
Username to run the application as.
Overridden by the
.Fl u
option.
Defaults to
.Cm fsunaba .
.It Ev FSUNABA_AMNESIC_USER
Username of the amnesic sandbox user used by the
.Fl A
option.
Defaults to the sandbox user name followed by
.Cm -amnesic ,
for example
.Cm fsunaba-amnesic .
.It Ev WIDTH
Sandbox display width in pixels.
Overridden by the
.Fl s
option.
Defaults to 1024.
.It Ev HEIGHT
Sandbox display height in pixels.
Overridden by the
.Fl s
option.
Defaults to 768.
.It Ev FSUNABA_TIMEOUT
Number of seconds to wait for
.Xr Xephyr 1
to become ready.
Defaults to 10.
.It Ev FSUNABA_XEPHYR_OPTS
Additional options to pass to
.Xr Xephyr 1 .
The
.Fl r
and
.Fl G
options append to this value.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
If the application exits normally, its exit status is returned.
Interruption by a signal exits with 128 plus the signal number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Open a
.Xr firefox 1
private browser window in a sandboxed display:
.Pp
.Dl $ Fsunaba firefox --private-window
.Pp
Run a command as a different sandbox user on a specific host display:
.Pp
.Dl $ Fsunaba -u alt-sandbox -d :0 xterm
.Pp
Run a browser with an empty, discarded home directory:
.Pp
.Dl $ Fsunaba -A firefox --private-window
.Pp
Run Tor Browser at its default window size:
.Pp
.Dl $ Fsunaba tor-browser
.Pp
Run a browser with a window manager in the sandbox, so its popup menus work:
.Pp
.Dl $ Fsunaba -w tor-browser
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr doas 1 ,
.Xr ksh 1 ,
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr xauth 1 ,
.Xr Xephyr 1 ,
.Xr XEvents 3 ,
.Xr X 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
is part of Flysunaba, a fork of
.Ox
Xsunaba, which in turn is based on a script by Milosz Galazka.
.Sh CAVEATS
Hardware acceleration is not available, so OpenGL applications fall back to
software rendering.
Applications using software rendering, notably
.Xr firefox 1
and Tor Browser, may print a
.Ic RenderCompositorSWGL failed mapping default framebuffer
warning, in particular while closing a window or a popup.
The warning is harmless and does not affect the application.
.Pp
The sandbox does not run a display manager or a window manager by default, and
does not execute the sandbox user's
.Pa ~/.xsession
or
.Pa ~/.xinitrc .
Use a wrapper script to configure and start an application that needs specific
environment setup.
.Pp
Without a window manager nothing moves the input focus off the pointer, so a
popup window that takes the focus away from the application window makes the
application close it: Firefox-based browsers, for example, close the hamburger
menu, the bookmarks and the security panel as soon as the pointer reaches them.
Running with
.Fl w
adds a window manager that manages the focus, which fixes those popups.
An application that instead expects to grab the pointer for its popups, such as
older Firefox-based browsers, cannot be fixed from outside and needs a version
where that path works.
.Pp
Setting the root cursor, as
.Nm
does, only removes the large X that a popup without a cursor of its own would
show; it does not keep such popups open.
.Pp
If another process takes the chosen display number before
.Xr Xephyr 1
starts,
.Nm
retries with the next free number.
The diagnostic output of such an attempt is captured and shown only when no
display can be started.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Nm
does not isolate the sandbox from the network.
An application with network access can exfiltrate any file the sandbox user can
read.
The application runs with the full privileges of the sandbox user, so it can
also write to its home directory and
.Pa /tmp ,
reach services on
.Cm localhost ,
read the sandbox user's
.Pa ~/.Xauthority ,
exhaust local resources, and reach
.Xr Xephyr 1
and the kernel through their own bugs.
.Pp
The X authentication cookie is passed to
.Xr xauth 1
through a pipe, so it does not appear in the process list where other local
users could read it.
.Pp
Audio and clipboard access are not shared by default.
Copying the invoking user's
.Xr sndio 7
cookie into the sandbox grants it access to the audio session, including the
microphone when kernel audio recording is enabled.
.Pp
The sandbox confines the application no further than the sandbox user, the
sandbox display, and the erased home directory do.
.Pp
Separate displays keep concurrent sessions apart in X, but two sessions that
run as the same sandbox user still share that user's home directory and
.Pa ~/.Xauthority ,
so each can reach the other's display.
Give each concurrent session its own sandbox user with
.Fl u
when they must not reach each other.
Amnesic mode wipes one shared home directory and is meant for one session at a
time.
.Pp
Amnesic mode erases the contents of the amnesic user's home directory, but the
directory itself is owned by that user and cannot be removed without root
privileges, and data written outside of it is left behind.
